+++ descriptive/Frontmatter/Abstract.tex
@@ -3,7 +3,7 @@
 
 A USB-C laboratory power supply based on an earlier DTU charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant converter was built and tested using the supplied planar magnetics. The controller was developed to handle a detachable load. Secondary voltage had to be regulated under load, while further charging had to be limited after disconnection because energy remained in the resonant tank and capacitors.
 
-The switching frequency was kept fixed at a nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}, and energy transfer was adjusted by changing the duration of gallium-nitride (GaN) half-bridge bursts with the microcontroller (MCU). Voltage feedback was converted into digital readings and transferred automatically to memory using direct memory access (DMA), avoiding repeated processor waits for individual measurements. The isolated secondary bulk bus was regulated using the final PWR-1.9.20 controller. Limited precharge was applied until feedback became available. Separate duty limits were used under load and at no load. The tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings are provided by a downstream output stage.
+The switching frequency was kept fixed at a nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}, and energy transfer was adjusted by changing the duration of gallium-nitride (GaN) half-bridge bursts with the microcontroller (MCU). Voltage feedback was converted into digital readings and transferred automatically to memory using direct memory access (DMA), avoiding repeated processor waits for individual measurements. The isolated secondary bulk bus was regulated using the voltage-control firmware. Limited precharge was applied until feedback became available. Separate duty limits were used under load and at no load. The tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings are provided by a downstream output stage.
 
 With an AC input recorded as \SI{240}{\volt}, \SI{50}{\hertz}, matched voltage and current readings at the \SI{20}{\volt} setting gave output powers of \SIrange{73.97}{79.89}{\watt} at the load. Average output voltage was \SIrange{19.973}{19.991}{\volt}, with recorded peak-to-peak ripple of \SIrange{0.416}{0.452}{\volt}. Logged power factor was 0.97--0.99 and current total harmonic distortion was \SIrange{2.23}{3.98}{\percent}. An initial efficiency estimate of \SIrange{76.5}{77.1}{\percent} was calculated from the paired input and output measurements. Uncertainty in their timing and calibration limits the accuracy of this estimate. Controller operation was recorded during the tested no-load and load-removal events. A thermal image labeled \SI{20}{\volt}, \SI{4}{\ampere} showed a maximum surface temperature of \SI{90.4}{\celsius}. Further loss measurements, improved heat removal, and protection-timing tests are still needed.
 
